<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RollForward>Major</RollForward>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <PublishAot>true</PublishAot>
    <!--
      PackTool packages whatever lands in the publish directory. For a native binary that would mean
      shipping a ~240 MB native PDB (plus the managed PDBs of assemblies that no longer exist as
      separate files) inside the tool package. The symbols are still produced next to the native
      binary for symbol publishing, they just don't get packed.
    -->
    <CopyOutputSymbolsToPublishDirectory>false</CopyOutputSymbolsToPublishDirectory>
    <ToolPackageRuntimeIdentifiers>win-x64;win-arm64;linux-x64;linux-arm64;osx-x64;osx-arm64</ToolPackageRuntimeIdentifiers>

    <!--
      The SDK refuses to build the RID-specific tool packages as inner builds for an AOT'd tool,
      because ILCompiler cannot compile across operating systems. CI therefore builds one of them
      per job on an agent of the matching OS, by passing Arcade's TargetRid - which also narrows
      what that job signs and publishes - while the rest of the repository stays RID-agnostic.
    -->
    <RuntimeIdentifier Condition="'$(TargetRid)' != ''">$(TargetRid)</RuntimeIdentifier>

    <!--
      Unlike `dotnet pack -r <rid>`, a RuntimeIdentifier on its own does not imply SelfContained,
      and the Native AOT publish then fails with NETSDK1102. This cannot be turned on
      unconditionally: SelfContained without a RuntimeIdentifier makes the SDK infer the host RID,
      which would turn every RID-agnostic build into a host-RID one and pack a RID-specific package
      in place of the wrapper.
    -->
    <SelfContained Condition="'$(RuntimeIdentifier)' != ''">true</SelfContained>

    <!--
      Newtonsoft.Json and YamlDotNet discover members purely through reflection, so the AOT analyzers
      flag every call into them (IL2026 "requires unreferenced code", IL3050 "requires dynamic code").
      The types they reflect over are rooted explicitly in ILLink.Descriptors.xml, which is what makes
      those calls work in the published native binary. IL2104/IL3053 are the per-assembly rollups ILC
      reports for dependencies we do not control (Octokit, NuGet.Packaging, the Azure DevOps client,
      ...). Only these codes are silenced; the trim-flow warnings (IL2070/IL2072/IL2091/...) remain
      build errors so genuinely unsafe patterns in our own code still fail the build.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);IL2026;IL3050;IL2104;IL3053</NoWarn>

    <ToolCommandName>darc</ToolCommandName>
    <Description>Darc CLI</Description>
    <PackageTags>Arcade Maestro Darc CLI Dependency Flow</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Maestro\Maestro.Common\Maestro.Common.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\ProductConstructionService\Microsoft.DotNet.ProductConstructionService.Client\Microsoft.DotNet.ProductConstructionService.Client.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DarcLib\Microsoft.DotNet.DarcLib.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="LibGit2Sharp" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console" />
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" />
    <PackageReference Include="System.CommandLine" />
    <PackageReference Include="YamlDotNet" />
  </ItemGroup>
  
  <ItemGroup>
    <TrimmerRootDescriptor Include="ILLink.Descriptors.xml" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A build emits either the RID-agnostic tool package or a RID-specific one, because the
    RuntimeIdentifier above makes the SDK produce the latter in place of the former. Shipping darc
    needs both: `dotnet tool install Microsoft.DotNet.Darc` resolves the RID-agnostic package, and
    that package is what redirects to the one matching the user's runtime identifier. A RID build
    therefore packs this project once more with TargetRid removed, which is also what ends the
    recursion - the inner build sees no TargetRid and so adds no reference of its own. The SDK
    does the mirror image of this in _CreateRIDSpecificToolPackages, where a RID-agnostic pack
    fans out to one pack per RID; it opts out for AOT tools, since AOT cannot cross-compile.

    The metadata is all load-bearing:
      - Targets/GlobalPropertiesToRemove are what make the reference pack rather than build, and
        with TargetRid gone.
      - NoBuild keeps it from compiling darc a second time. The RID-agnostic package carries no
        implementation, only tools/any/any/DotnetToolSettings.xml naming the per-RID package ids,
        so the SDK already drops Publish from the pack (see _PackToolPublishDependency), and this
        drops the Build that NuGet's Pack would otherwise force.
      - Private stops consumers of darc from collecting publish output through this reference;
        without it, ProductConstructionService.ScenarioTests sees darc's files under both its
        RID-specific and RID-agnostic paths and fails with NETSDK1152.

    Only one build should pack it, since every RID build otherwise would and only one copy ships.
    win-x64 is the one that does, because that is the leg which already builds and publishes the
    rest of the repository.

    NuGet builds its restore graph out of @(ProjectReference) with no regard for
    ReferenceOutputAssembly, and walks it by recursively invoking a target on each reference with a
    fixed set of global properties, so a self-reference is a genuine cycle there and restore fails
    with MSB4006. ExcludeRestorePackageImports is the property NuGet puts on every evaluation in
    that walk and on nothing else, so hiding the reference from it keeps restore to the real graph.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetRid)' == 'win-x64'
                        and '$(ExcludeRestorePackageImports)' != 'true'">
    <ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildProjectFullPath)"
                      Targets="Pack"
                      GlobalPropertiesToRemove="TargetRid"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      Private="false"
                      SkipGetTargetFrameworkProperties="true"
                      AdditionalProperties="NoBuild=true" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Microsoft.DotNet.Darc.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Microsoft.DotNet.DarcLib.Codeflow.Tests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DynamicProxyGenAssembly2" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
